WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.697 --> 00:00:07.030
MATT THOMAS: Hi, my name's Matt.

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:08.830
And I'm head of
foresight and insight

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:10.870
at the British Red Cross.

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:13.990
Cast your mind back
to March of 2020,

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:17.470
what now feels like
several lifetimes ago.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:20.260
The COVID-19 pandemic was
starting to grip the world.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.960
And the UK, where
I am, was entering

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.180
its first national lockdown.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.390
The British Red Cross was in the
midst of planning our emergency

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:29.590
response.

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:32.110
We were gearing up to
deliver food parcels,

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:34.540
and deliver medicines,
support people

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.420
who were lonely, or
socially isolated,

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:39.580
and help people
who had been moved

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.310
into emergency accommodation.

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:45.310
But, how did we know
who needed support

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:48.590
and where we might find them?

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:52.490
The day before lockdown
began, I made our first commit

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:56.360
to what would become known
as the vulnerability index.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.540
My colleague, Al, and I,
were rapidly designing a map

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.090
to model vulnerability
to COVID-19

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:05.090
in neighborhoods across the UK.

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:08.570
This map would be used to
plan not only the British Red

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:11.540
Cross's pandemic
response, would also

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:16.280
inform other voluntary and
community sector organizations,

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:20.870
as well as government
and public health bodies.

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:25.370
It needed to be expensive,
rigorous, and accurate.

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:28.830
And we needed to
build it quickly.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:32.130
700 commits, more
than a hundred data

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:35.160
indicators, and
several new gray hairs

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:38.440
later, this is where we are now.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.260
My team and I built a
set of open-source tools

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:44.830
that measured the vulnerability
of different geographical areas

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:48.100
of the UK and their
ability to cope.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.460
Today, we have a suite of
dashboards, maps, and data sets

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.250
that anyone can use for free.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.910
How did we get there?

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:59.280
We had to figure out
what makes people

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.970
vulnerable to the disease.

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:05.600
COVID-19 isn't just a
public health emergency.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.000
It's also a social and
economic emergency.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.090
And we needed to account
for vulnerability

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:14.440
in a multi-dimensional way.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:18.280
We gathered data on over a
hundred indicators related

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:20.470
to people's clinical
vulnerability,

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:24.580
and socioeconomic vulnerability,
as well as their wider health

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.770
and well-being needs.

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:29.860
But, we had to find some
way to merge everything

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.470
into a single
vulnerability score,

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:35.350
and we needed to do it fast.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:39.693
So I picked up the
phone and I called Mike.

00:02:39.693 --> 00:02:40.735
MIKE PAGE: Hey, I'm Mike.

00:02:40.735 --> 00:02:43.900
Head scientist at the
British Red Cross.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:45.940
After agreeing to
redeploy into Matt's team,

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:48.760
I took a quick peek at the
repositories he had created,

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.430
excited to see what I
might be working on.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:54.721
My reaction was,
of course, panic.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:01.430
What I thought was going to
be a project in its infancy,

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:02.960
had already morphed
into thousands

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.690
of lines of code, split
across hundreds of files,

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:08.090
data sets, repositories.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.280
Add to this the pressure
of a pandemic, and the need

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:14.700
to develop rapidly, I wondered
what I got myself into.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.200
Thankfully, I was soon to learn
that these feelings of panic

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:18.700
were unfounded.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:21.070
The project had a silent hero
working in the background,

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:23.350
allowing me to quickly
get up to speed.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:25.000
This hero was the Tidyverse.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.660
So, why the Tidyverse?

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.970
Every data size problem
presents a unique landscape,

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.530
which must be navigated.

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:37.290
Here the British Red Cross, we
like to think of the Tidyverse

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:41.400
as a cognitive superhighway
for many of these landscapes.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.260
The signs are clear
and easy to follow.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:44.670
And it's easy to
show colleagues,

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:49.150
and your future self, how to
navigate through the terrain.

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:51.730
But, this is not to say
the Tidyverse is always

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:54.160
the correct path to
take for every problem.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:56.860
Depending on the task at hand,
other tools and paradigms

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:00.460
may be better suited, such
as pandas and datatable.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:01.960
However, at the
British Red Cross,

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:04.210
we often have a requirement
to develop and collaborate

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:04.930
quickly.

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:08.560
And this is why we believe the
Tidyverse shines most brightly.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:11.530
It quickly allows us to
translate our ideas to code.

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:17.209
This is because the
unifying design principles

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:19.700
of the Tidyverse are
centered first and foremost

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:25.080
around cognitive performance,
not computational performance.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:26.843
To demonstrate why
this is important,

00:04:26.843 --> 00:04:28.260
I'm going to show
you some example

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.960
code snippets from the
vulnerability index Matt

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.433
introduced earlier.

00:04:33.433 --> 00:04:34.850
For each snippet,
I'll demonstrate

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:36.920
how the unifying
principles of the Tidyverse

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:38.877
made development
quicker and easier,

00:04:38.877 --> 00:04:41.210
allowing us to respond rapidly
to the emerging pandemic.

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:48.010
First, let's take a look at the
human-centered and inclusive

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:49.120
principles.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:51.160
Together, they acknowledge
that data scientists

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.450
come in all shapes and sizes.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:55.522
This means the Tidyverse
APIs and documentation

00:04:55.522 --> 00:04:56.980
are designed to be
read and written

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:00.220
by everyone, irrespective
of their background.

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:02.680
This means it's not just for
computer science graduates,

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:04.603
whose idea of a fun
weekend is arguing about

00:05:04.603 --> 00:05:06.520
whether a raise should
be zero or one indexed.

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:13.990
This quote from the Tidyverse
design principles book

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:18.070
sums up the human-inclusive
principles nicely.

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:21.700
"One particularly powerful
idea is that of affordance.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:26.250
The exterior of a tools
should suggest how to use it."

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:30.030
For example, take a look at this
code snippet, written by Matt,

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:32.640
that calculates a mean total
response time from the fire

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:36.701
and rescue authorities, using
a classic dplyr style pipeline.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:45.460
Without ever having seen the
code, and only a little bit

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.920
of prior Tidyverse
knowledge, it's quick to read

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:51.970
and intuitive to understand
what is going on.

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:55.880
Take the fire and
rescue authority stats.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:58.190
Filter only for fires
in English dwellings.

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:00.920
And create a new
variable, called "year."

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:03.180
So, it's only the
variables of interest.

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:05.090
And, finally, group
by geographical

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:07.970
variable to find the average
three-year fire and rescue

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:11.620
response time in a given area.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.800
The code almost reads
like English prose,

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:15.795
listing off a set of
clear instructions.

00:06:15.795 --> 00:06:17.170
There's no
requirements to reason

00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:19.430
about things the
computer's optimized to do,

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:21.670
such as indexing and subsetting.

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:23.950
The result is code that
is quicker to write, read,

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:25.390
and collaborate on.

00:06:25.390 --> 00:06:28.120
Another important design
principle of the Tidyverse

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:30.310
is that of consistency.

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:33.040
Principally, this
idea means finding

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:34.810
the smallest possible
set of key ideas,

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:37.300
and using them again and again.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.640
This manifests itself in
two ways in the Tidyverse,

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:41.410
in its structures
and function APIs.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:45.780
The code snippet
I'm about to show

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:48.820
demonstrates the power
of this idea effectively.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:50.800
This code generates a
dataframe of weights,

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:53.200
across four domains of
vulnerability, where only two

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:55.660
domains should be weighted
at any given time,

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:58.910
and the sum of the
weights must equal one.

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:01.400
The main power of the snippet
lies in the first function,

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:04.880
crossing, which generates
all combinations of variables

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:07.190
found in the data set, while
duplicating and sorting

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:07.690
the inputs.

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:15.400
Here, the yellow arrows
demonstrate the consistent Tidy

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:19.030
dataframe structures used
throughout this example.

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:22.240
Using one common data
structure over and over again,

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:23.710
reduced the cognitive
load required

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:27.220
to reason about the problem
we were challenged with.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:30.160
One reason for this is that
the consistent data structures

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:33.280
mean the APIs of the Tidyverse
communicate seamlessly.

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:40.180
For example, we
can see, here, how

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:44.150
the functions from tidyr and
bplyr fit together like a box.

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:45.820
This mostly removes
the need to reason

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:47.320
about the input and
output structure

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:49.720
of the data being passed
throughout of this Tidyverse,

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:54.580
making it quick to learn new
tidy functions and packages.

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:57.100
Consistency also extends
to the naming of functions

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:58.510
throughout the Tidyverse.

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:00.310
In general, Tidyverse
function names

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:02.290
offer imperative
verbs over nouns,

00:08:02.290 --> 00:08:04.540
long names over short
names, and thematic unity

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:06.735
between similar
groups of functions.

00:08:06.735 --> 00:08:08.110
The end result
are functions that

00:08:08.110 --> 00:08:10.550
easy to remember and implement.

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:13.570
This particular code snippet,
I recall a deep sense of joy,

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:15.640
as I intuitively knew
the name of the functions

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:18.560
I needed to recall, despite
only using several of them

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:20.230
a handful of times.

00:08:20.230 --> 00:08:22.060
The final design
principle of the Tidyverse

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:23.650
is that it should be composable.

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:25.330
This means a complex
problems to be

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:27.910
solved by combining
many small pieces,

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:30.490
with each small piece
easy to reason about.

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:31.900
This is realized
in the Tidyverse

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:34.419
for a predominant functional
programming paradigm,

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:36.490
that uses immutable objects.

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:39.070
This means problems are solved
by combining functions that

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:41.919
transform data, rather
than by creating objects

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:44.680
whose state changes over time.

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:46.447
We saw the power of
this, idea previously,

00:08:46.447 --> 00:08:48.280
where a consistent,
immutable Tidy dataframe

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:50.110
object was used, throughout.

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:53.220
This long codeblock demonstrates
the idea of compatibility,

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:53.720
nicely.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:00.650
Try not to be drawn in to
immediately understand it.

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:03.770
Instead, notice how many
small pieces of the tiny bits

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:07.160
are used to solve an overall,
more complex problem.

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:09.620
In some instances, some
functions, such as mutate,

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:11.480
are used over and over again.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:14.750
The end result is we are able
to solve complex problems more

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:15.250
quickly.

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:20.270
And this particular
case allowed us

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:23.585
to assess the vulnerability of
different regions of the UK,

00:09:23.585 --> 00:09:25.250
split across different
vulnerabilities

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:27.950
of the vulnerability index.

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:30.910
So, let's turn back
to Matt and find out

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:33.850
how these design principles
have impacted our response

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:36.466
to the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:09:36.466 --> 00:09:38.690
MATT THOMAS: Have you noticed
how all the principals

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:40.460
are linked together?

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:42.470
The Tidyverse
principals are rooted

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:44.510
in cognitive theories of mind.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:47.810
And they make it easy for
humans, like me and Mike,

00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:49.610
to pull insights
out of data sets

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:52.630
and make new tools for people.

00:09:52.630 --> 00:09:55.900
One example of how we've
used our vulnerability index

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:58.510
to design better
services for people

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:01.630
is our COVID-19 hardship fund.

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:05.290
Aviva, an insurance company,
donated five million pounds

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:08.530
to us, to give to people who
are facing economic hardship

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:10.540
during the pandemic.

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:14.050
We used our vulnerability
index, alongside other research,

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:15.610
to figure out that
the people most

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:19.660
likely to need financial support
included those without access

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:23.530
to state welfare, older
isolated people, people

00:10:23.530 --> 00:10:27.010
facing homelessness or living
in temporary accommodation,

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:30.850
and survivors of
gender-based violence.

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:33.730
In one region of
England, this mapping,

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:37.210
combined with local intelligence
from our frontline teams,

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:40.570
led to more than half of
referrals to our hardship fund

00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:43.480
coming from the most
vulnerable areas.

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:47.470
As of December 2020, we've
helped more than 7,000 people

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:50.710
with financial assistance.

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:52.390
So, following the
Tidyverse design

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:55.630
principles made it faster
for Mike and I, and others,

00:10:55.630 --> 00:10:57.760
to get our ideas
out of our heads,

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:01.810
and translated into something
useful in the real world.

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:04.210
This cognitive speed
is really key for us,

00:11:04.210 --> 00:11:07.030
working as a humanitarian
organization.

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:09.850
To put a spin on the
well-known agile phrase,

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:12.550
"We need to move
fast and save thing."

00:11:12.550 --> 00:11:14.830
And we're not just
working with one another.

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:17.470
It is often said, your
most important collaborator

00:11:17.470 --> 00:11:21.220
is yourself, six
months down the line.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:23.680
Even if you don't use
the device itself,

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:26.380
we suggest using all
four design principles

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:30.280
as inspiration for future
package and code design.

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:32.590
Writing code and
packages in the future,

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:34.600
think about cognitive
performance.

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:37.240
Not only computational
performance.

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:39.100
For our work on the
vulnerability index,

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:41.140
this meant that there
hasn't been a single time

00:11:41.140 --> 00:11:44.270
that Mike or I had to explain
our code to one another,

00:11:44.270 --> 00:11:46.210
which is remarkable.

00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:47.860
We should often be
writing code that

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:50.920
is human-centric and designed
around how people actually

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:52.270
think and work.

00:11:52.270 --> 00:11:55.390
The key is to build tools
and analysis faster,

00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:57.590
for real world impact.

00:11:57.590 --> 00:11:59.790
Thank you for listening.

